{"lecture_id":"2026-07-08-M2-2","lecture_summary":"The lecture connects torque-based balance and center of mass to continuous mass distributions, then develops moment of inertia, rotational kinetic energy, direct rod integrations, the parallel-axis theorem, and additive inertia for composite objects.","videos":[{"rank":1,"title":"Center of Mass Physics Problems - Basic Introduction","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2uszSnvzBEU","reason":"Builds the mass-weighted-position and balance intuition needed before the lecture’s continuous center-of-mass calculations."},{"rank":2,"title":"Inertia - Basic Introduction, Torque, Angular Acceleration, Newton's Second Law, Rotational Motion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ic_wFYu8xVs","reason":"Provides the strongest conceptual treatment of rotational inertia, its dependence on mass distribution and axis choice, and its relationship to torque and angular acceleration."},{"rank":3,"title":"Parallel Axis Theorem & Moment of Inertia - Physics Practice Problems","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JrkimXqnCLw","reason":"Directly teaches the shifted-axis method used to obtain and verify the lecture’s end-axis and off-center rod results."}],"coverage_gaps":["No strong playlist match covers the variable-density workflow dm = λ(x)dx, normalization of λ(x) = Cx from total mass, and integration yielding x_cm = 2L/3.","No listed video directly derives rod moments of inertia by integration, including I_cm = ML²/12, I_end = ML²/3, the variable-density result ML²/2, or the axis-at-L/3 result ML²/9.","No direct match covers adding the inertia of a rod and an attached point mass about a common axis.","The closest rotational-kinetic-energy video focuses on rolling bodies, inclines, and pulleys rather than this lecture’s derivation of K_rot = Iω²/2 and rod examples, so it was omitted from the minimal set."]}
